  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 1.42,
      "text": " You felt your phone vibrate."
    },
    {
      "start": 1.94,
      "end": 2.44,
      "text": " You checked it."
    },
    {
      "start": 3.0,
      "end": 3.28,
      "text": " Nothing."
    },
    {
      "start": 4.02,
      "end": 5.88,
      "text": " So what did you actually feel?"
    },
    {
      "start": 6.52,
      "end": 8.86,
      "text": " Sometimes the phone never vibrated at all."
    },
    {
      "start": 9.58,
      "end": 12.08,
      "text": " That experience is called a phantom vibration."
    },
    {
      "start": 13.52,
      "end": 16.32,
      "text": " One proposed explanation starts with what you expect."
    },
    {
      "start": 17.08,
      "end": 18.24,
      "text": " When you're waiting for a call,"
    },
    {
      "start": 18.76,
      "end": 20.6,
      "text": " your brain may interpret another sensation"
    },
    {
      "start": 20.6,
      "end": 22.0,
      "text": " as the familiar buzz."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.8,
      "end": 25.5,
      "text": " Pressure from clothing or a small muscle movement"
    },
    {
      "start": 25.5,
      "end": 26.62,
      "text": " are possible candidates."
    },
    {
      "start": 27.540000000000003,
      "end": 30.96,
      "text": " But researchers haven't pinned down exactly what causes it."
    },
    {
      "start": 31.56,
      "end": 33.38,
      "text": " Surveys document the experience."
    },
    {
      "start": 33.98,
      "end": 35.16,
      "text": " They don't prove that mechanism."
    },
    {
      "start": 36.14,
      "end": 38.52,
      "text": " So a convincing buzz isn't always proof"
    },
    {
      "start": 38.52,
      "end": 39.88,
      "text": " that your phone vibrated."
    },
    {
      "start": 40.52,
      "end": 44.14,
      "text": " The sensation can feel real, even when the alert wasn't."
    }
  ]
}
